# Next.js / Nuxt coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to the two
meta-frameworks this pack covers: Next.js (App Router, React) and Nuxt
(Vue 3). Applies to `.tsx`/`.jsx` (Next.js) and `.vue` (Nuxt) files — a
project using only one of the two frameworks still gets guidance scoped to
that framework from the sections below; a monorepo with both gets both.

## File and directory naming

- Next.js App Router: route segment folders are `kebab-case`
  (`app/order-history/page.tsx`); reserved file names (`page.tsx`,
  `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `route.ts`) stay exactly as the
  framework requires them — do not rename them for house style.
- Nuxt: page files under `pages/` are `kebab-case` and map to routes by
  file-system convention (`pages/order-history.vue` ->
  `/order-history`); a dynamic segment is `pages/orders/[id].vue`, a
  catch-all `pages/[...slug].vue`. Component files are `PascalCase.vue`.
- Composables (Nuxt) and custom hooks (Next.js/React) both start with
  `use` (`useOrderTotals.ts`, `useOrderTotals`), one export per file for a
  non-trivial composable/hook.

## Component and directive placement

- Next.js: every component file is a Server Component by default; add
  `'use client'` as the FIRST line of a file only when that file itself
  needs browser state (`useState`, `useEffect`), event handlers, or a
  browser-only API — not because an ancestor or descendant needs it. Keep
  the directive on the smallest leaf component that actually needs it.
- Nuxt: use `<script setup lang="ts">` for new single-file components
  instead of the Options API or a bare `<script>` with `defineComponent` —
  it is the current idiomatic form and gives auto-imported
  `ref`/`computed`/`watch` without explicit imports.
- Type props explicitly: Next.js components take a typed `props` object
  (an interface or inline type, not `any`); Nuxt SFCs declare props with
  `defineProps<{ ... }>()` (the type-only generic form), not the runtime
  object form, for anything beyond a trivial component.

## Imports and auto-imports

- Nuxt auto-imports composables from `composables/`, components from
  `components/`, and utils from `utils/` — do not hand-import something
  Nuxt already auto-imports (it adds a redundant, easily-stale import
  line); DO explicitly import anything outside those conventional
  directories.
- Next.js has no auto-import convention — import explicitly, using the
  project's configured path aliases (`@/components/...`) instead of long
  relative chains once the project has one configured.

## Formatting and typing

- Format with the project's configured formatter (Prettier is standard for
  both ecosystems); do not hand-format around one that is already
  configured.
- Avoid `any` for props, route params, and fetch responses in either
  framework — type route params from Next.js's generated `PageProps`
  types (or the project's own param type) and Nuxt's `useRoute().params`
  by name, not by casting.
- Server-only code (`'use server'` files, Nuxt `server/` routes) and
  client-only code should not share a barrel import file that re-exports
  both — that is how a server-only secret or a `node:` import ends up
  bundled into client JavaScript.
